2026-09-22 01:42:29 [DEBUG] llm.engine: ARGS: ('\n        You are a workflow assistant. Extract parameters for the intent "select_idea_by_reference".\n\n        User message:\n        select this -> 23-719 trump v anderson\n\n        Parameter schema for this intent:\n        - "reference": type=string, required=True\n\n        Extraction instructions for this intent:\n        - The "reference" parameter must always be set to the idea\'s "id" field.\n- Never return the idea name or description.\n- Use the relevant context block (which lists ideas with id, name, and description)\n  to determine which idea the user is referring to.\n- If the user refers to an idea by name, description, or position (e.g., "the immigration case",\n  "idea 3", "the fed reserve governor case"), resolve it to the correct idea and return its id.\n\n\n\nRelevant context:\nideas:\n  - id: 23-1007, name: Cunningham v. Cornell University, description: The case concerns a dispute over whether Congress intended for plaintiffs to plead and prove liability under ERISA, or if defendants can claim exemptions based on information that plaintiffs cannot know prior to discovery.\n  - id: 24-1287, name: Learning Resources, Inc. v. Trump, President of U.S., description: The Supreme Court is considering whether President Trump had the authority to impose tariffs under a law granting him broad powers in foreign emergencies, as he claims is necessary to address the country\'s economic and national security threats.\n  - id: 25A312, name: Trump, President of U.S. v. Cook, description: The case concerns the removal of a Federal Reserve governor due to alleged deceit or gross negligence in financial transactions, specifically regarding mortgage applications and interest rates.\n  - id: 24A884, name: Trump v. CASA, Inc., description: The case concerns the constitutionality of Executive Order 14,160, which protects American citizenship, and the use of universal injunctions by district courts to block government actions.\n  - id: 25-332, name: Trump, President of United States v. Slaughter, description: The case concerns the President\'s power to remove and supervise executive branch officials, with Petitioners arguing that a Supreme Court decision known as Humphrey\'s Executor should be overturned because it allows federal agencies to wield significant power without being accountable to the President.\n  - id: 23-719, name: Trump v. Anderson, description: The case concerns whether a former president who incited an insurrection against the Constitution can be disqualified from public office under Section 3 of the 14th Amendment, which prohibits oath-breaking insurrectionists from holding federal power.\n  - id: 19-635, name: Trump v. Vance, description: The case concerns whether a county district attorney can issue criminal process against a sitting President of the United States, with the Supreme Court to decide if temporary presidential immunity is constitutionally required. The issue at hand involves a subpoena for the President\'s tax returns, with implications for presidential power and local accountability.\n  - id: 20-1029, name: Austin v. Reagan National Advertising, description: This case, Austin v. Reagan National Advertising, involves a dispute over the interpretation of content-based regulation under the First Amendment and whether certain sign codes are constitutional.\n  - id: 23-939, name: Trump v. United States, description: The case concerns whether a sitting president can be prosecuted for their official actions, with the petitioner arguing that such prosecution would undermine the presidency and the separation of powers.\n  - id: 22-324, name: O’Connor-Ratcliff v. Garnier, description: The case O\'Connor-Ratcliff v. Garnier concerns whether public officials retain their First Amendment rights when acting in a personal capacity, specifically when blocking users from their private social media pages.\n  - id: 22-704, name: Vidal, Under Sec. of Comm. v. Elster, description: The case concerns the constitutionality of a federal law restricting trademark registration for marks referring to living individuals without their consent, with the Petitioner arguing it is viewpoint neutral and does not restrict speech.\n  - id: 20-366, name: Trump v. New York, description: The case concerns the President\'s authority to determine how to count individuals in the country illegally for purposes of apportioning congressional seats, with the President arguing he has discretion to decide who is a resident and should be counted.\n  - id: 19-715, name: Trump v. Mazars USA, LLP, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether Congress has the authority to subpoena personal records of a sitting President, with implications for the balance of power between the legislative and executive branches.\n  - id: 22-1078, name: Warner Chappell Music, Inc. v. Nealy, description: This case is about whether a copyright plaintiff can recover damages for acts that occurred more than three years before filing suit, and whether the discovery rule applies to extend this period.\n  - id: 19-547, name: United States Fish and Wildlife Serv. v. Sierra Club, Inc., description: The Supreme Court is considering whether the government must disclose internal documents related to a decision made in 2014 about the impact of a proposed action on endangered species. The documents were written before the final decision was made and may not have been adopted by the decision-makers.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\n}\n',)
